  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n\nToday (Friday) & Tomorrow (Saturday)...\n\nA passing upper-level trough today (Friday) will soon bring \nheight/pressure rises to the area with a ridge expected to follow in \nbehind from the west. This feature will lead to regional subsidence \nthat will both clear out skies and suppress any chance for \nprecipitation through the weekend and for the first half of next \nweek. At the surface, a massive near 1045 hPa high pressure center \nwill continue to build in from off the High Plains/Rockies. As the \nsurface pressure continues to rise today, winds speeds out of the \nnorth should only continue to slow down through the afternoon/night \ntonight. Light to calm winds (speeds <10MPH) could return as early \nas this evening. As this high pressure center passes through the \narea tonight, the light surface winds will swap to a south to \nsouthwesterly orientation ahead of Saturday. \n\nThe main story tonight, however, will be for sub-freezing \ntemperatures as lows settle in the upper 20s to low 30s across the \nentire region. As this will become the areas first freeze of the \nseason, a Freeze Warning will be in effect from 1AM to 9AM \ntonight/Saturday morning. Though the warning does not go into effect \nuntil 1AM tonight, a few areas northwest of the Tri-Cities could \npossibly see temperatures drop quickly into the mid to low 30s \nshortly after sunset (common underneath light winds and clear \nskies). This freeze should only remain a one night issue as the \nsoutherly returning surface winds for Saturday (10-20 mph gusting as \nhigh as 30-35 mph in the afternoon) should bump up temperatures \naround 10 degrees higher than today. Highs for Saturday will look to \nrange the upper 50s to low 60s with overnight lows in the mid 30s to \nlow 40s. \n\n\nNext Week...\n\nConsiderable confidence from the LREF members regarding the upper-\nlevel pattern next week lead us to conclude that this ridging \npattern will continue on into the first half of next week. This \nfeature will allow temperatures to retain in the 60s through Monday \nwith a more notable warmup possible Tuesday and Wednesday. The long-\nrange models continue to also keep the surface winds out of the \nsouth with little day-to-day change of surface broad high pressure \nto the east and broad low pressure to the west (channeling in the \nsoutherly warm air advection pattern). \n\nThe next chance of precipitation does not come in the forecast until \nThursday as the next upper-level trough/wave looks possible to slide \nin next. Though model guidance begins to wane in regards to the \ntiming/intensity of the trough, at least some chance of \nprecipitation looks to be plausible as this next disturbance \napproaches near the middle-to-end of next week. Details will continue \nto be refined as this event becomes closer into range.",
